[SIZE=5][B]The RIPPCSX2 menu[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][LEFT][B]Run ELF, Run Last ELF:[/B] rebuild, press, look.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Lab Monitor:[/B] the video mode, PMODE, SMODE, DISPFB and DISPLAY, and the breakpoint's state and hits, live.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Test Steps:[/B] a list of waits, button presses, screenshots and recordings, played into what is running.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Compatibility Report:[/B] the game, what started it, the result, the OPL modes and the machine's state, as a file and a row in a CSV.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]HDTV Output, EE Data Write Breakpoint:[/B] the fork's two big changes, each one click from stock behaviour, and the breakpoint now switches while a game runs.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Lab Switches, Lab Folders, Open Log:[/B] everything a test run needs in one place.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]

[SIZE=5][B]BIOS and storage: where it is strong[/B][/SIZE]
It runs your own BIOS dump, so the region, the ROM version and every step from power on are the real ones.
[LIST]
[*][LEFT][B]ROM 1.60 (SCPH-39001, USA)[/B] is the one most tested. [B]ROM 2.20 (SCPH-77001, USA)[/B] boots to the console's menu and card browser. ROM 2.30 and later have not been tested.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]A virtual internal hard drive, exFAT,[/B] is the strongest path: RIPPS2 and stock OPL both list games from it and start them.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]Memory cards as files,[/B] disc images, and an ELF's own folder through host:.[/LEFT]
[*][LEFT][B]PS1 games through Ember.[/B] Digimon World's intro runs here through RIPPS2 and Ember, with your own PS1 BIOS on the drive.[/LEFT]
[/LIST]
[SIZE=2]Not there yet: USB drives, MMCE, SMB and an APA hard drive have not been run here. MX4SIO and i.LINK stop a run, so turn them off for now.[/SIZE]
[CENTER][IMG]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/akilluminati47/RIPPCSX2/rippcsx2/docs/release/ember-ps1.jpg[/IMG][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B]Linux[/B][/SIZE]
New with this release: an x64 AppImage, built by the same workflow as the Windows zip. It builds, but we have not run it on a Linux machine yet, so if you try it, please tell us what happens. The taskbar button for hidden runs is Windows only.

[SIZE=5][B]The goal: the whole library[/B][/SIZE]
The biggest job for RIPPCSX2 is one nobody has finished: testing PS2 games through Open PS2 Loader's own code path, and writing down what each one needs. OPL does not run a game the way a disc does. It patches the kernel, swaps the IOP's drivers and answers for the drive, and some games need one of its compatibility modes to survive that. Stock OPL starting a game unmodified was the first step. Mode patching, KELFs and a shared list of every game are next.

